Statements:
[
    "Sleep quality is a multidimensional construct encompassing subjective satisfaction, sleep efficiency, latency, duration, and disturbances.",
    "Poor sleep quality is consistently associated with impairments in memory consolidation, attention, executive function, and processing speed.",
    "Neural mechanisms linking sleep and cognition include synaptic homeostasis, memory consolidation during sleep stages, and glymphatic clearance of metabolic waste.",
    "Older adults experience changes in sleep architecture and cognitive declines, creating a bidirectional relationship where sleep disturbances exacerbate cognitive aging.",
    "Sleep interventions are potential cognitive enhancers, particularly for age-related cognitive decline.",
    "Current research is limited by reliance on subjective sleep measures, varied cognitive assessment tools, and a lack of longitudinal studies.",
    "Sleep constitutes approximately one-third of human life and serves essential functions for restoration and cognitive maintenance.",
    "Approximately 30-48% of adults report poor sleep quality, with the prevalence increasing with age.",
    "The Pittsburgh Sleep Quality Index (PSQI) is the gold standard for subjective sleep quality assessment.",
    "Objective sleep measurement approaches include polysomnography (PSG) and actigraphy.",
    "Discrepancies exist between subjective and objective sleep measures, which show only moderate correlations.",
    "Sleep efficiency is more strongly associated with cognitive performance than total sleep time alone.",
    "Sleep fragmentation is particularly detrimental to memory consolidation and executive function.",
    "Slow-wave sleep (SWS) is important for declarative (fact-based) memory consolidation, while REM sleep contributes to procedural (skill-based) memory.",
    "Attention is one of the most sleep-sensitive cognitive domains, with sleep restriction causing cumulative deficits.",
    "The synaptic homeostasis hypothesis proposes that slow-wave sleep facilitates synaptic downscaling to optimize neural networks for learning.",
    "The glymphatic system, a brain-wide waste clearance pathway, becomes significantly more active during sleep.",
    "Sleep deprivation reduces glymphatic clearance, potentially contributing to the accumulation of neurotoxic proteins like beta-amyloid.",
    "Sleep regulates neuroendocrine systems, including growth hormone and cortisol, and immune functions related to inflammatory cytokines.",
    "Normal aging involves characteristic changes in sleep architecture, such as reductions in SWS and sleep efficiency.",
    "Clinical sleep disorders like insomnia and obstructive sleep apnea (OSA) are associated with distinct cognitive impairment profiles.",
    "Treatments such as CPAP for OSA and CBT-I for insomnia can produce cognitive benefits.",
    "Sleep extension and optimization are promising strategies for cognitive enhancement.",
    "The review identifies several gaps in current literature, including a lack of population diversity (WEIRD bias), limited longitudinal designs, and measurement inconsistencies."
] 

